really  participate hard  make a lot of things happen uh and the  the challenge there is  is that both of them sort of want the  same thing or they're both happy with  the same thing they're happy with low  cost  offsets and a lot of them  ultimately what needs to be what needs  to happen is systemic  change you know we're sort of nibbling  around the edges with some of these  different things  we need to change how we produce energy  we need to change how we transport  ourselves we need to change  what and how we eat you know we we need  systemic change associated with  transitioning to a low carbon  economy a lot of what we're doing today  is not promoting systemic change it's  sort of trying to stick your  your thumb in the dike to preserve the  current system  and and at the end of the day that can't  really  [Music]  work  [Music]  coming up environmental destruction  harms us all  but could the people behind it soon be  tried alongside war criminals  a panel lawyers thinks they should and  is drafting a new definition  of ecocide to do so this  is bloomberg green  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  from bloomberg's headquarters in new  york i'm kaylee lynes and this  is bloomberg green well global  environmental rules are patchy at best  which means holding multinational actors  to account can be tricky  even when companies are taken to court  fines are often absorbed and business  continues  but now a panel of lawyers is drafting a  definition of ecocide  to submit to the international criminal  court in hopes that raising  environmental destruction to the level  of genocide  will reign in some of humanity's worst  excesses  this decade is going to be decisive in  how we  approach dealing with the climate crisis  dealing with the ecological crisis  um and dealing with ecocide is a bit  like going to the root cause so climate  change if you like  it is a symptom and you know mass damage  and destruction of ecosystems is  actually one of the root causes  when bp spilled 210 million gallons of  oil into the mexican gulf in 2010  it wasn't just seabirds and dolphins  that were the victims  ten years on the natural resources  defense council estimates that the  overall cost to fisheries in the gulf  was 8.7 billion dollars  with a loss of more than 20 000 jobs  white house energy advisor carol browner  called it the worst environmental  disaster the us  have faced bp was hit with a record fine  of 20 billion dollars  but deep water drilling continues to  this day  a growing number of people think that  the courts did not go  far enough damage to nature is simply  not taken seriously enough  as we can see our economic system is set  up around maximizing profit  and doing so within certain ground rules  like you know you don't kill anybody  um but those ground rules don't include  serious damage to nature and we're  seeing on an ever more apocalyptic scale  what the result of that is  so what if there was a different way  what if  instead of shrugging off fines as  exceptional expenses  decision makers could be tried alongside  war criminals  and those who committed genocide  it may sound extreme but that's what  supporters of eco side law  want the international criminal law is  really about  human survival and human dignity it's  very much  focused on human race and we have  genocide elimination of a human group  or crimes against humanity what could be  more of an international crime  in other words a crime that no matter  where it occurs on the planet affects  all of us  than destruction of our very environment  kate mcintosh and jojo meta are part of  a panel of lawyers  currently drafting legislation to be  presented to the international  criminal court the best chance yet for  ecocide to be made into law  it's a long road to ratification but  supporters include french president  emmanuel macron greta toomberg and even  pope francis so what we're wanting  corporations to see this as is a  guardrail but also as a challenge  you know it's like you know what we want  we want those ceos to be saying  you know i can see this coming down the  line and it's very sensible it's  ultimately it's almost like a health and  safety regulation for the planet  you know how do we comply with that new  health and safety regulation we we want  to be ahead of that curve  because we want to be placed in a way to  take things forward in a way that is  sustainable and that is actually helping  the planet to thrive  rather than destroying it as we go  the lawyers pursuing ecocide believe it  must  be on a level with genocide or crimes  against humanity  it might be hard to imagine ceos in the  dock alongside  dictators and despots but it's hope the  threat of prosecution will be enough to  change behaviour  we're not talking about genocidal  maniacs  or warlords that are trying to win  territory we're talking about  basically reasonable people who are  going about their business  and i can't imagine that those people  would feel comfortable  about being potentially prosecuted as an  international criminal  in the same way that they might feel  quite comfortable about  dealing with fines that would result  from civil suits brought against them  despite optimism and widespread public  support  ecocide laws are at least a few years  off  even if the icc accepts the new  definition of ecocide  one of its 123 member states will still  need to propose it  and the rest will have their turns  squabbling over the details  only then could enforcement begin  lawyers estimate that could take up to  four years  but that might be just what businesses  need to get their houses  in order of course there will need to be  a transition period i'm not gonna  you know make any bones about that you  know if we brought this law in tomorrow  it would be chaos  it's not about an instant change that's  gonna somehow you know dump everybody  somewhere that they're completely  unfamiliar and don't know what to do  this is about looking at something  that's coming down the line because  something like this will have to come  sooner or later  we're looking at something coming down  the line within the next few years  that inspires compliance pathways  transition policies  you know all of these kind of things  that will need you know adjustments that  will need to be made  but will need to be made with a very  different future in mind a future that  we all know we have to approach  the power of ecocide law lies in  criminalizing the destruction of nature  on an individual level next time a  company causes an oil spill its decision  makers  could be facing jail time and that  personal accountability  is what its supporters say will drive  the change  effectively what you're looking at is an  individual  being held to account because they're in  a decision-making position  within that project and that's precisely  again where the power comes in  because it's easy to hide behind the  corporate veil and have your company  fine for something you made a decision  for but once  that sort of protection is removed  you're going to think a lot harder it's  a serious concentration for the mind  that's it for this week's edition but  keep the conversation going  follow us on twitter at climate i'm  kaylee lyons in new york  and this is bloomberg green
 

